# Neutralize direct-access serving of anything under /uploads/.
#
# Attackers (or a customer that inbound-uploaded media, or a workspace
# manager who uploaded an auto-reply catalog) could craft a file whose
# magic bytes look like a PDF but whose original filename ends in .html
# or .svg. mime_content_type() in PHP passes the whitelist, and the
# file is saved keeping the .html extension. Apache would then serve it
# with text/html and the browser executes the embedded <script> in the
# portal's origin - stored XSS with the session cookie in reach.
#
# Two independent defenses below:
#   1. Never execute server-side scripts under /uploads/, period.
#   2. Force EVERY file to download rather than render inline, and
#      strip inference-based Content-Type sniffing.
#
# Files legitimately displayed inline (agent-uploaded chat images shown
# in the composer preview) go through /api/media.php which sets its
# own headers - direct hits to /uploads/... always download.

# 1. No server-side execution under /uploads/.
<FilesMatch "\.(?:php|phtml|phar|phps|py|pl|rb|cgi|jsp|asp|aspx|sh)$">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# 2. Force browsers to treat everything under /uploads/ as a download.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    Header set Content-Disposition "attachment"
    Header set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
    Header set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'none'; sandbox"
</IfModule>

# Belt-and-braces: strip active markup rendering regardless of the
# request's Accept header.
<FilesMatch "\.(?:html?|xhtml|svg|xml|xsl|xslt)$">
    ForceType application/octet-stream
</FilesMatch>

# Directory listing off.
Options -Indexes
